{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Inverse","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.braid_eigenphase_quantized","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.braid_eigenphase_quantized","kind":"theorem","line":217,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/EigenphaseQuantization.lean#L217-L226","doc":"**The corpus's Part-1 conclusion, as one theorem.**  A `q`-uniform\nbraid-image eigenphase on `V_(2,1)` — i.e. one whose character is a constant\ninteger — lies in `{0, π/3, π/2, 2π/3, π}`.\n\nThis is the `d = 2` case and it needs no Kronecker step; see the section\ndocstring above. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.phase_quantized","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.phase_quantized","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/EigenphaseQuantization.lean#L188-L215","doc":"**The five-element phase set.** On `[0, π]`, the five quantised cosine\nvalues pin `α` to exactly `{0, π/3, π/2, 2π/3, π}`.\n\n`Real.injOn_cos` is what makes this an equality rather than a containment. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.not_braid_eigenphase_of_two_cos_mem_Ioo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.not_braid_eigenphase_of_two_cos_mem_Ioo","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/EigenphaseQuantization.lean#L141-L153","doc":"**The required ladder phase is not a `q`-uniform braid-image eigenphase.**\n\nGiven only that `2 cos α` lies strictly between `1` and `2` — which is what the\nmeasured `α_req` satisfies, `2 cos α_req = 1.99725…` — the phase cannot be the\neigenphase of any braid-image matrix on `V_(2,1)` that is elliptic uniformly in\n`q`, because such a character is a constant *integer*.\n\nNote this covers a **constant** `α` as well as a `q`-varying one, which is\nexactly the case the boundedness-only argument leaves open. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.not_isInt_of_mem_Ioo_one_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.not_isInt_of_mem_Ioo_one_two","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/EigenphaseQuantization.lean#L130-L139","doc":"A real strictly between two consecutive integers is not an integer.\n\nThis is the exclusion mechanism: the required phase has\n`2 cos α_req = 1.99725…`, strictly inside `(1,2)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.two_cos_eq_int_cases","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.two_cos_eq_int_cases","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/EigenphaseQuantization.lean#L107-L128","doc":"**Quantization of a `q`-uniform braid-image eigenphase on `V_(2,1)`.**\n\nA character of the braid image lies in `ℤ[q,q⁻¹]`; if it is additionally bounded\non `(0,∞)` it is a constant integer, and on the two-dimensional irrep the bound\nis `2`.  So `2 cos α` is an integer in `[-2,2]`, leaving five possible cosines. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.cos_rung_lt_of_base_short_ladder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.cos_rung_lt_of_base_short_ladder","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/EigenphaseQuantization.lean#L98-L105","doc":"The same statement with the damping factor written as `1 - u^2`, the shape\nthe committed ladder actually uses at `c₂ = -1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.cos_rung_lt_of_base_short","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.EigenphaseQuantization.cos_rung_lt_of_base_short","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/EigenphaseQuantization.lean#L78-L96","doc":"**A bounded multiplicative rung cannot lift a base that already falls short.**\n\nIf the base `Qbase`, already damped by the positive `N = 2` factor `d`, sits\nbelow the target, then no choice of phase `α` rescues it: the `N = 1` rung is a\ncosine, and `cos α ≤ 1`.\n\nInstantiated on the ruled `z_D` base this is the statement that deriving the\neigenphase cannot close the `Q_β` gap — the gap is in the base. ","signatureChunk":0}]}